{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-120432",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268510/assets/f07b2120c62162bb1ce4697126d24f000d78f0032d9fa63e8576585342f30535.png)\n\nLet's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. An icosahedron is a regular polyhedron that \n\n- consists of $20$ congruent faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle, and\n\n- exactly $5$ faces meet at each vertex.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since there are $F=20$ faces each in the form of a triangle, we have\n\n$$\nS=20\\cdot 3=60\n$$\n\nsides combined. Now, each edge of the polyhedron is created when two face sides coincide. So, the number of edges of the icosahedron must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{S}{2}=\\frac{60}{2}=30.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting the number of edges $E=30$ and the number of faces $F=20$ into Euler's formula, we can solve for the number of vertices $V$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-E+F & =2 \\\\ V-(30)+(20) & =2 \\\\ V-10 & =2 \\\\ V & =12 \\\\  & \\ne 14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
